# Fail Closed — No Fallbacks

- Fallbacks are prohibited unless the user explicitly requires that exact fallback as product behavior.
- Missing, malformed, ambiguous, rejected, or uncorrelated data must produce a clear hard failure.
- Never guess identifiers, fuzzy-match results, choose the latest resource, reuse older output, change formats, or silently substitute defaults after an operation fails.
- Preserve exact identity and correlation across request ids, jobs, generators, indexes, assets, posts, hashes, and provider responses.
- Fix the upstream operation, contract, parser, or validation instead of hiding the defect downstream.
- Exceptions and provider errors must remain failures through logs, archives, APIs, and UI state.
- If an explicitly required fallback exists, document it at the call site and independently prove it cannot select unrelated data.
- Defaults chosen before an operation starts are configuration. They must never be used to replace failed or missing output after execution begins.
- Scope (owner, 2026-10-06): apply these checks at trust and version boundaries. These are provider and external replies, requests that reach the server, and stored records.
- This server and its browser code ship together. Do not add page code that re-checks this server's own replies. Do not plan for this project's own code to break; fix shared code at its source.
